function scheduleLogs() {
  let i = 0; // was: var i = 0;
  while (i < 3) {
    setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
    i++;
  }
}

scheduleLogs();

explanation:
Closures capture **bindings** (variables), not the values they held at creation time. What makes `let` "fix" loops is not the keyword itself but where it is declared:

- `for (let i = 0; ...; i++)` is special: the loop creates a new `i` binding for every iteration and copies the current value into it, so each callback gets its own `i`.
- Here `let i` runs once, before the `while`. There is exactly one `i`, and all three callbacks share it. By the time the timers fire, the loop has incremented it to `3`.

The same applies to a `for` loop whose head doesn't declare the variable (`let i; for (i = 0; i < 3; i++)`): still one shared binding.

Fix: give each iteration its own block-scoped copy, or use a `for (let ...)` head:

```js
while (i < 3) {
  const n = i; // new binding each time the body runs
  setTimeout(() => console.log(n), 0);
  i++;
}
```

